7}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1eb730-a0c6-5c06-9968-e7a8bed24e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "≤" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0729bd0-95a3-5521-bc42-e53d65fdb2e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e53c3cdf-7677-5e74-84b5-4fbd3ffc112e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181172" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/5c4ffbb64a754df9a6100fda3fd6eaf4a68f28ac59de4dfb716c2c8b1f2847ab.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8984067f-ee63-5c27-ae87-df28015e26fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "≠" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195290} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ce2b38-eb4a-537e-98c0-bccbdcf8fc99" 